references/fdr/accessible-bathroom-and-grab-bar.md:78:**Sources:** Threshold OT (OT home assessment blog, US, Co-2); Mattress Miracle accessibility guide (CA, Co-2); ADA Lodging Best Practices Bed Height Fact Sheet (US, Tier 5); Accora FloorBed product/clinical documentation (UK, Co-2)
references/fdr/accessible-bathroom-and-grab-bar.md:82:| Bed transfer height — wheelchair user | 430–585mm AFF (17–23 inch range); matched to WC seat height | Transfer from WC to bed requires surface height match; above or below this range makes independent transfer extremely difficult or impossible | ADA Lodging Best Practices / Corada (US) | Tier 5 | **NOVEL** — specific transfer-height range with WC-match rationale absent from BPC |
references/fdr/accessible-bathroom-and-grab-bar.md:86:| Mattress firmness — transfer surface | Firm/medium-firm preferred; avoid high-sink memory foam | Deep-sink mattresses make edge-of-bed repositioning and transfer initiation significantly harder; OT consistently recommends against high-sink surfaces for transfer-dependent users | Threshold OT (US); ADA Lodging Best Practices | Co-2/Tier 5 | **NOVEL** — mattress firmness as OT-specified transfer parameter absent from BPC |
references/fdr/fdr-p1-granular-runs-2026-04-09.md:36:| Contrast at counter edges | LRV ≥50 at counter edge for partial sight | Consistent with CON-0043 (≥50% best practice). Prevents hand/arm contact with hot surfaces. | **CONFIRMS** C-04 (≥50% already in guidebook as best practice) |
references/fdr/fold-down-grab-bar-specification.md:50:**Clinical reasoning:** For unilateral UPL the design problem is laterality-dependent in a way it is not for bilateral users. The fold-down bar position must be specified relative to the user's functional side, not as a population-average bilateral provision. This is the OT reasoning: assess which side is functional → specify bar on that side. For a bilateral household, a fold-down on each side (one deployed for the current user, both available for future occupants) is the DAR best practice.
references/fdr/fold-down-grab-bar-specification.md:61:| Slip-resistant surface requirement | "Slip-resistant" surface mandatory (NBC Canada 2015); ADA does not mandate surface type but mandates presence of bar adjacent to wet plumbing fixtures | Wet areas; reduced grip — Canadian code more specific than ADA; "satin-finish, slip-resistant" now acceptable specification language replacing legacy "peened" or "knurled" | NBC 2015; ADA §609 | Tier 5–6 | CONFIRMS presence of bar; REFINES by updating surface specification language |
references/fdr/luminance-contrast-lrv-evidence-base.md:17:| Large adjacent surfaces — reduced threshold | ≥20 LRV acceptable for large areas where illuminance >200 lux | VI: large surfaces at high illuminance are easier to discriminate at lower contrast — 20 LRV is a reduced threshold acceptable in specific conditions, not a general relaxation | SATRA LRV guide (BS 8300 Annex B) | Tier 5–6 | **NOVEL** — conditional 20 LRV threshold for large surfaces at high illuminance absent from BPC |
references/fdr/luminance-contrast-lrv-evidence-base.md:18:| Small items (handles, switches, sockets) — higher contrast | ≥15 LRV for door handles vs door face; though small items can be acceptable with smaller differential due to 3D nature | VI: 3D small items (handles) are visible through shadow and shape as well as contrast — BS 8300 allows smaller differential than flat surfaces; however maximum contrast is always safer | SATRA LRV guide; Gradus guide | Tier 5–6 | **NOVEL** — small-item LRV threshold distinction absent from BPC |
references/fdr/luminance-contrast-lrv-evidence-base.md:19:| TGSI (integrated tile type) | ≥30% luminance contrast | VI: integrated tile TGSIs have larger contact area — lower threshold acceptable | AS 1428.4.1:2009; CAG; ESP Australia | Tier 5–6 | CONFIRMS (cross-reference FDR-VI-01); jurisdictional synthesis |
references/fdr/mobility-built-environment.md:7:**Primary sources:** Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (Aragon & Kings, 2010); Ramos et al. 2020 systematic review [PMC7683074, DOI: 10.3233/JPD-202035] (Tier 2)
references/fdr/mobility-built-environment.md:13:**Sources:** Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (Aragon & Kings, 2010) [Co-2]; Ramos et al. 2020 SR [Tier 2]
references/fdr/mobility-built-environment.md:17:| Floor visual cue strip — length | ≈450mm; high-contrast to floor surface | Freezing of gait (FOG) / festination in corridor; placed parallel at approximately individual stride-length intervals in known trouble zones | Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (Aragon & Kings, 2010) | Co-2 | **NOVEL** — PD-specific floor cue strip specification absent from BPC |
references/fdr/mobility-built-environment.md:18:| Visual cue strip — corner treatment | Fan pattern of strips around 90° turn; same ≈450mm strip length | FOG at direction changes; strips must be within visual field during use | Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (2010) | Co-2 | **NOVEL** — corner-specific strip layout absent from BPC |
references/fdr/mobility-built-environment.md:20:| Stair nosing lines — gait cueing effect | Stair nosings function as natural visual gait cues; stair negotiation is rarely problematic for PD users (unlike level corridors) | PD with FOG; stair vs. level-surface distinction | Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (2010) | Co-2 | **NOVEL** — stair/corridor distinction absent; informs why flat corridors need embedded cues but stairs typically do not |
references/fdr/reach-range-and-accessible-controls.md:78:**Clinical reasoning note (Petito case):** Community OT/wheelchair specialist documents that WC assessment must include home environment measurement — counter height is determined by wheelchair geometry, not by population average. This is the clinical reasoning basis for why adjustable worktops are best practice, not optional.
references/fdr/residential-entry-and-threshold.md:20:| Handrail — residential ramp | Required when ramp rise >152mm (6 inch) or horizontal run >1828mm (72 inch); bilateral preferred even when not required | WC, fatigue, ambulant: bilateral handrails allow load-sharing and provide access regardless of affected side; residential code allows unilateral but OT best practice is bilateral | ADA §405.8; National Ramp; OT clinical reasoning | Tier 5–6/Co-2 | CONFIRMS handrail; **NOVEL** — bilateral preferred even when not required, with clinical reasoning absent from BPC |
references/fdr/residential-kitchen-and-task-surfaces.md:36:| Tap/faucet type | Single-lever or sensor/touch; long lever arm; no separate H/C controls | Unilateral UPL — simultaneous hold and twist is not possible; single lever operable with wrist or elbow | AOTA; Neurolutions; RCOT (UK) | Co-2 | CONFIRMS single-lever; REFINES by adding sensor/touch as best practice and elbow-operability rationale |
references/fdr/stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility.md:40:**Sources:** Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (Aragon & Kings, 2010) [cross-reference FDR-MOB-01]; PMC7683074 (Ramos SR); Physio-pedia PD gait
references/fdr/stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility.md:44:| Stair step edge — natural visual cue for PD | Stair nosing lines function as natural, effective visual gait cues for PD users — stair negotiation is rarely a serious FOG problem unlike level corridors | PD festination/FOG: the horizontal line of each step nosing provides the same visual cueing function that artificially placed floor strips provide on level ground; stairs are therefore often safer than expected for PD | Parkinson's UK / COT OT Best Practice Guidelines (2010) | Co-2 | **NOVEL** — stairs as naturally cue-rich environment for PD (safer than level corridors) absent from BPC; inverts expectation |
references/fdr/threshold-door-hardware.md:76:| Door weight — fatigue populations | ≤22.2 N for door opening force; automatic operator best practice for severe fatigue | CFS/ME, MS: energy envelope is fixed and finite — repeated heavy door operation depletes it; automatic door preserves energy for functional tasks within the home | RCOT HAwD 2019; OT clinical reasoning | Co-2 | CONFIRMS force limit; REFINES with fatigue-specific energy-conservation rationale |
references/fdr/upper-limb-impairment-built-environment.md:62:| Bed surface height — WC transfer | 430–585mm AFF to mattress top; optimal = level with WC seat height (within 50mm) | SCI WC user: height mismatch >50mm increases transfer difficulty and shoulder injury risk; downhill transfer easier than uphill | CORADA best practices; MSKTC Safe Transfer Technique | Tier 5/Co-2 | REFINES — adds SCI-specific level-transfer principle and directional preference |
references/methodology-evidence-hierarchy-mapping.md:33:| Tier 6 | Statutory codes | Legally enforceable accessibility codes. **Code-baseline citations only** — the regulatory floor, never a best-practice anchor |
references/methodology-evidence-hierarchy-mapping.md:39:- **Tiers 4–6 are the regulatory stratum,** walled off from best-practice anchoring: convergence of standards/codes is convergence-not-evidence (`tier-system.md` §3). A T4/T5 source can support a best-practice claim only where its own evidence basis is documented as traceable to T1/T2 (re-graining rule, `governance/evidence-architecture.md` §4 G1, PROPOSED).
references/methodology-evidence-hierarchy-mapping.md:58:| Tiers 4–6 | Not classifiable | Not classifiable | GRADE does not rate consensus standards; the project classifies them explicitly — as a regulatory stratum, not as best-practice evidence |
references/methodology-evidence-hierarchy-mapping.md:165:v1's two-marker system is superseded: it allowed "Tier 4–6 only → ●" with the rationale that "code compliance is inherently evidence-based (the evidence being the code itself)." That rationale is the convergence-laundering failure mode `tier-system.md` §3 names, in its oldest recorded form. Under v2, a T4–6-only basis renders ◐/○ and — per the PROPOSED `governance/evidence-architecture.md` G1 (pending owner ratification) — would be flagged `regulatory_stratum_only` in the determination table and carry the regulatory-stratum register language in every audience rendering, never best-practice language (invariant I3).
references/methodology-evidence-hierarchy-mapping.md:182:| Standards/codes | Not addressed | Not addressed | Not addressed | Explicit regulatory stratum (T4–6), walled off from best-practice anchoring |
