references/project-standards.md:4814:## Owner rulings 2026-10-30 — best practice, the specification page and rendering (from the ramp-slope session)
references/project-standards.md:4818:On best practice and the specification:
references/project-standards.md:4820:- "best practice = the best way to do something according to how it benefits the intended group"
references/project-standards.md:4821:- "best practice =/= most common or agreed upon. we have been through this a million times"
references/project-standards.md:4822:- "remember that our guidebook doesn't take positions. it just presents what appears to be the most accommodating/gentle best practice for anything... so unless an academic article or some professional body improves upon germany's standard, that's identified as the best practice"
references/project-standards.md:4826:- "We are interested in what are best practices NOW, and that means we do not need to interrogate the history of anything but rather the current state of everything"
references/project-standards.md:4840:- "i want it to be coming from database directly in ideal world"
references/project-standards.md:4847:- "note: all work must comply with website accessibility standards and best practices"
references/project-standards.md:4853:1. **Best practice is the most accommodating value found, not the commonest.** The value a determination names as best practice is the gentlest or most accommodating one found in practice, in the direction recorded for the parameter (`base_parameters.accessibility_direction`; for ramp gradient, lower is better), unless a study or a professional body improves on it. It is not the modal or the agreed value, and the steepest value a code allows is not highlighted. No study is needed for a value to be named, and a T4 to T6 value is accepted as a baseline without tracing its history. [ASSUMPTION: this ruling selects which value is named; it does not change the band or the flag. Under the 2026-07-21 owner directive (Option A, `governance/tier-system.md` section 8, `governance/evidence-architecture.md` I3), a determination resting on T4 to T6 alone is already stated as weak-band (○) "best practice as currently known", flagged weak and code-derived, with the convergence-not-evidence caveat. Read together, the gentlest code value is named best practice at ○ with the flag. The ramp session's page marked such values ● and called the conflict "D-DOCT"; on this reading there is no conflict to record, the ● is not carried, and no `SUPERSEDES` line is written. If the owner means to drop the ○ band or the flag, that is a further ruling and needs a `SUPERSEDES` against `governance/tier-system.md` section 8 and `governance/mission-and-epistemics.md` section 2.]
references/project-standards.md:4860:8. **Rest landing is the second headline parameter for ramp gradient.** The owner's hypothesis is that the rest landing, not the slope, is the problem, and the owner agreed to add landing spacing as a second headline parameter. Which value is best practice follows item 1: the shortest run between rests found. The ramp session's reading of that figure (Germany, 6 m, from a secondary source) is unverified here.
references/project-standards.md:4861:9. **Two statements are not settled and are not applied as rulings.** (a) A row with no value is not shown, and `governance/mission-and-epistemics.md` requires a pending cell to display `[BEST-PRACTICE-PENDING]` with a gap link. [ASSUMPTION: the two are met together by showing no row and stating the gap, with its link, in the page's Gaps section; no `SUPERSEDES` line is written.] (b) The question line under the title is irrelevant, and the mission document makes questions first-class data. [ASSUMPTION: "irrelevant" refers to the single question line the ramp page carried, not to the questions-led commitment; how questions appear on a specification page is left to the owner.]
references/project-standards.md:4865:CONDITION: any session asking what counts as best practice for a parameter, whether a T4 to T6 value may be named best practice, which value to highlight on a page, what "provisional" or "covered" means, how a specification or item page is rendered or what it must contain, whether the page has an audience lens, what the accessibility bar for a rendered page is, which jurisdictions a page shows, or whether rest-landing spacing is a parameter.
references/project-standards.md:4866:ACTION: (1) Name as best practice the most accommodating value found in the parameter's recorded direction, never the commonest and never by highlighting the steepest code value; keep the ○ band and the weak, code-derived flag for a determination resting on T4 to T6 alone, and do not mark such a value ●. (2) Describe a reading as provisional until a second reading of its source confirms it, and as covered only when read, extracted and verified. (3) Render a specification page from the database, not from a committed file, and take its element-to-table mapping from the render contract; do not restore a static generator. (4) Offer the audience and disability lenses, and write nothing as accessible that has not passed a recorded test. (5) Show only in-scope jurisdictions and no row without a value, stating the gap under Gaps. (6) Mint rest-landing spacing as a parameter through the sanctioned CLI in a data PR of its own, with its direction recorded from an admitted source and not hand-set. (7) Treat every [ASSUMPTION] mark above as unratified.
references/project-standards.md:4877:1. **Item 1 of the previous entry is ratified.** A determination resting on T4 to T6 alone keeps the weak-band (○) and the weak, code-derived flag with the convergence-not-evidence caveat, under `governance/tier-system.md` section 8 and `governance/evidence-architecture.md` I3, as amended 2026-07-21. The owner's best-practice statements of 2026-10-29 and 2026-10-30 decide which value is named, and do not change the band or the flag. Nothing is superseded, so no `SUPERSEDES` line is written. [ASSUMPTION: "keep" confirms the position of the previous entry's item 1 in full, including that a value of this kind is not marked ●, which the ramp-slope session's page had done.]
references/project-standards.md:4881:CONDITION: any session asking whether a gentlest code value named as best practice carries the ○ band and the flag, or whether the ramp-slope session's ● for such a value stands.
references/project-standards.md:4894:1. **Supersession.** None is claimed. The statement agrees with the ratified posture, so there is no `SUPERSEDES` pair. [ASSUMPTION: it is read as reaffirming the posture and not as retiring any of the ratified texts that use the phrase "best practice" with a marker (`governance/evidence-architecture.md` section 6, the Core Doctrine marker rule in this file, `governance/mission-and-epistemics.md`). If the owner means it to reach those, they have not said so, and that needs a decision record, not a session's reading.]
references/project-standards.md:4991:specifications: must be able to review all evidence and syntheses of evidence to determine what best practice is, which explicitly means the most accommodating/gentle/dignified set of values/rationales/solutions for a given specification item within a category. the most commonly seen value/etc is NOT evidence of best practice but most common practice. must be able to understand code is a floor unless backed up directly by high value evidence, and we are more likely to see best practices coming from academic research/professional clinical guidelines/advocacy groups.
references/project-standards.md:4997:"if code documents are the only things that inform a specification, then they can be best practice...it's just going to be a weaker evidence tier"
references/project-standards.md:5100:[ASSUMPTION: "translate it all properly" is read as OR-12b's generation of in-use, jurisdiction-specific terms for flagged searches, and not as licence to store translations as vocabulary; the owner's earlier OR-17 ("It is not acceptable for the model to generate its own phrases/names/aliases") and E4 are not read as withdrawn.]
references/project-standards.md:5266:**What it does not decide.** The relation types, their representation, who judges them, and how a dynamic run is initiated are open. It does not change the rule that only an Opus-class model authors `best_practice_synthesis`. [ASSUMPTION: "synthesis" is read as the pipeline stage of that name, not as `best_practice_synthesis` alone.]
references/project-standards.md:5409:**What decided it.** Criterion 1 (integrity): the determination key is ruled (2026-08-26, 2026-08-28) and a per-jurisdiction determination would change it and count adoptions as findings; filing an Ontario or Flemish instrument under its country misstates provenance. Criterion 2: one best practice with the jurisdictions beside it is what the owner asked for on 2026-10-30. Criterion 3: climate and culture are conditions some groups' provisions turn on (the 2026-04-07 thermoregulation and Kawa rules; `governance/armature_v4_resolutions.md` Q29).
references/project-standards.md:6250:> **Q1.** "best practice is the best/most forgiving/etc of all values/approaches for a given term. isn't it presumptuous to have a best practice column at this stage? differentiate as code floor vs above code floor"
references/project-standards.md:6260:**What Q1 states as a rule.** Two of its sentences state a rule rather than ask: "best practice is the best/most forgiving/etc of all values/approaches for a given term" restates the 2026-10-30 rulings that best practice is the most accommodating value for the intended group, an output of the determination and not a label on an input; and "differentiate as code floor vs above code floor" adds an instruction. [ASSUMPTION: the instruction is read as — derive floor and above-floor against the code stratum of the same parameter and jurisdiction, never type it on a figure; record a source's own "code" marking as root attribution (the code's `ref_id` when held, else `untraced` with the marking quoted as the classification basis) and the source's own pairing of a higher figure with a code figure as a relation edge; and store no "best practice" on any input row — basis: the adjudication of Q1, `scratchpad/session_2026-11-13-edmonton-checklist-pass/ADJUDICATION.md` section 1, which rests on OR-19's presumed echo and rule 8; unratified.] The question in Q1 ("isn't it presumptuous…") was a question about a column of the digest's report, never of the schema.
references/project-standards.md:6572:> "yes convergence is possible too, and it helps define most common practice (which isn't same as best practice)"
references/project-standards.md:6574:[ASSUMPTION: "convergence is possible too" selects the item's second offer: agreement between any two kinds of evidence counts as convergence, symmetric with the owner's OR-6 and OR-6c use of "divergent" for any two tiers, and including the item's own examples of what could until now only disagree ("two research studies, or a research study and a professional guideline") — basis: the item offered exactly this as the alternative to leaving it, and "too" answers its "as you did for disagreement"; unratified.] [ASSUMPTION: the other conditions of the written definition and of the OD-43 delegated resolution stand, because the owner's words remove only the limit to the clinical and Co-1 axes: the evidence states the same value or the same range on the parameter's scale; the anchoring claims can all hold (OR-6b: differing floors that can all be met are not divergent, which is not agreement); and the governing figures rest on two or more independent roots, sources that share a root counting as one source (H1: `grep -n -F 'precedent counts documents; evidence counts roots' governance/evidence-architecture.md`) — basis: the answer names none of them; unratified.] [ASSUMPTION: "it helps define most common practice (which isn't same as best practice)" is read as: a convergent assessment states the most common practice (the value or range the agreeing evidence holds), and a convergence output is never labelled, stored or rendered as best practice; best practice stays the most accommodating value found in the parameter's recorded direction — basis: the owner's own parenthesis, and the owner's earlier words `grep -n -F 'best practice =/= most common or agreed upon' references/project-standards.md` (2026-10-30) and `grep -n -F 'is NOT evidence of best practice but most common practice' references/project-standards.md` (2026-11-06); unratified.]
references/project-standards.md:6580:OD-43: agreement between any two kinds of evidence counts as convergence; convergence defines most common practice, which is not best practice
references/project-standards.md:6613:ACTION: (1) T-068 carries answer 1: `convergent` on agreement between any two kinds of evidence, with the remaining conditions, its output read as most common practice and never labelled or rendered as best practice; T-037 and T-032 carry the render note. (2) T-070 carries answer 2: option A, the source its section 3 query prints, retrieved first; the owner still launches the raters. (3) T-091 and T-092 carry answer 3: building use and interior or exterior only; the headline measurement counts those two and is shown to the owner before any re-tag. (4) Answer 4 is carried by OD-56, resolved under delegation and independently checked before any task builds its mechanism; meanwhile no research batch declines a provision of presence. (5) Read OD-43 and OD-44 through the gate; OD-49 and OD-53 keep their status lines, in the scope this entry gives them. (6) Treat every [ASSUMPTION] above as unratified.
